# BotTrunk

> A marketplace where AI agents pay per call, in USDC on Algorand over x402 (HTTP 402 Payment Required), for services that include work done by real people. No API keys, no accounts, no subscriptions: the payment is the credential.

What makes this catalog different: besides data utilities, an agent can buy work from a person. `deposit-bac-*` puts money into a person's bank account within 24 hours and returns the bank's receipt reference (live corridor: BAC Credomatic accounts, paid in lempiras).

## How to call anything here

Every service is `POST https://api.bottrunk.com/s/<slug>` with a JSON body.

1. Call it with no payment header. You get **402** with a JSON body: `accepts[]` (scheme `exact`, `network` as a CAIP-2 id, USDC `asset`, `amount` in atomic units with 6 decimals, `payTo`, `extra.feePayer`), a `resource` object and an `extensions.bazaar` block carrying the input/output schema. The same body is base64 in the `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header.
2. Sign a USDC transfer for exactly `amount` and retry with the `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` header (`X-PAYMENT` is accepted for v1 clients).
3. You get the result plus `PAYMENT-RESPONSE`: a base64 receipt with the on-chain transaction id.

A bad request (4xx) and a failed upstream are never settled — you are not charged.

**What your wallet needs.** Build the transfer as an atomic group naming `extra.feePayer` and the facilitator covers the network fee, so the call costs you USDC only; sign a plain single transfer instead and you pay its ~0.001 ALGO. Either way the paying account must be opted in to USDC asset 31566704, and an Algorand account holding an asset needs about 0.1 ALGO of minimum balance. Send **0.3 ALGO once** (0.1 to exist, 0.1 to hold the asset, the rest for fees), then USDC per call. An agent funded with USDC and no ALGO cannot pay at all, and USDC sent before the opt-in does not arrive — it fails. `bottrunk-mcp` opts in by itself once the ALGO lands.

## What you get back, and whether it costs you

| Status | `error` in the body | Charged |
|---|---|---|
| 200 | — | yes, `PAYMENT-RESPONSE` carries the transaction id |
| 402 | `Payment required` | no — you sent no payment header |
| 402 | `missing or malformed X-PAYMENT header`, `unsupported x402 version`, `scheme mismatch`, `network mismatch`, or the facilitator's own `invalidReason` | no |
| 402 | `settlement failed` | no — verification passed and the work ran, but the transfer did not settle |
| 422 | a validation message (`url must be a public http(s) URL`, `url resolves to a private address`, `selector "…" matched nothing on this page`, …) | no |
| 502 | `fetch failed with N`, `page too large`, `fetch failed: …` | no |
| 503 | `<name> is not live yet`, with `status` and `catalog` | no — listed for discovery, not callable |

**Retry rules.** A 402, 422, 502 or 503 means nothing was settled, so retrying is free. The one to handle carefully is `settlement failed`: the service already did the work, and if the transfer did land on-chain despite the error, signing a fresh payment pays twice. Check the payer address on an explorer before re-signing, or call `GET /orders/{order_id}` for human-fulfilled services. Everything else is safe to retry.

**Timeouts.** Allow at least 30 s per paid call: fetching plus verify plus settle has measured around 4.6 s end to end, and built-in services themselves time out at 20 s. The per-service latency published on the site measures fulfilment only, not settlement.

**Rate limits.** None published today. Be reasonable; abusive traffic gets blocked at the edge.

## MCP

**Remote, nothing to install:** `https://mcp.bottrunk.com/mcp` — MCP over Streamable HTTP, no key, no account. Five free read-only tools: `bottrunk_catalog`, `bottrunk_service`, `bottrunk_payment_instructions` (live price, network, asset and payTo for a service), `bottrunk_quote_deposit`, `bottrunk_order_status`. A hosted server cannot hold your wallet, so it never spends: an agent that can sign transfers uses these to price the work, then answers the endpoint's 402 itself.

**Local, with a wallet:**

    npx bottrunk-mcp wallet   # creates the agent's own wallet, prints the address
    # send it 0.3 ALGO, then the USDC it may spend; the opt-in happens by itself
    claude mcp add bottrunk -- npx -y bottrunk-mcp

Every live service below becomes a paid tool, with per-call and per-day spending caps you control. Package: https://www.npmjs.com/package/bottrunk-mcp
Setup for Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, Cline, Windsurf, Zed, OpenClaw, Hermes Agent, Goose, the OpenAI Agents SDK and LangChain: https://bottrunk.com/connect

## Services

Every built-in service shares these limits: pages over 2 MB are refused (`page too large`), connect times out at 5 s and the whole fetch at 20 s, at most 3 redirects are followed, requests go out as `BotTrunk/0.1 (+https://bottrunk.com/docs)`, hostnames resolving to private/loopback/link-local space are refused with 422 before any request is made, and the endpoints are POST-only. Per-service behaviour is in `behaviour[]` on https://api.bottrunk.com/api/v1/catalog/{slug} — read it before you pay, it is the part that is not in the schema.

<% @services.each do |service| %>
- **<%= service.slug %>** — <%= service.summary %> <%= service.lempira? ? "Priced from the Banco Central de Honduras reference rate (about #{usdc(service.price_atomic, decimals: 2)} for L#{number_with_delimiter(service.price_hnl)} today)." : "#{usdc(service.price_atomic)} USDC per call." %><%= " Not callable yet — write to hello@bottrunk.com to arrange it." if service.on_request? %> Endpoint: <%= service.endpoint_url %>
<% service.behaviour.each do |label, body| %>  - <%= label %>: <%= body.delete("`") %>
<% end %><% end %>

